{% extends 'dashboard/help/_topic.html.twig' %}
{% block content %}
<p>A client may only send with a From address at a <strong>sending domain</strong> it has proven to control, and in production only once the domain also has an active DKIM key. The account's admin sets a domain up in the account's dashboard, under Settings › Sending domains, without the operator. Each domain has its own page that shows one step at a time, with copy buttons for every record: <em>prove it is yours</em>, <em>publish its signing key</em>, <em>ready to send</em>.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Prove it is yours.</strong> An admin adds the domain (<em>Add a domain</em>), within the account's limit of sending domains (its own limit, never above <code>APP_CLIENT_SENDING_DOMAIN_LIMIT</code>); connecting an application that names its domain adds it too. An operator can also run <code>smarthost:domain:add</code>. The page shows a TXT record: <code>_smarthost-verification.&lt;domain&gt;</code> with a random token.</li>
<li><strong>Publish its signing key.</strong> When the record is found, the domain is verified and its DKIM key is requested from the host agent (the service on this server that runs the production tools). The agent makes the key inside OpenDKIM with a new selector (<code>cm</code>, the year and month, and four random characters); the private key never leaves the OpenDKIM container. The page then shows the DKIM record to publish.</li>
<li><strong>Ready to send.</strong> When the published DKIM record matches the key, catto-mail asks the host agent to activate the selector. The agent compares DNS with the private key again and activates it (<code>smarthostctl prod dkim activate</code>: OpenDKIM reloads its tables without a restart, so no mail is refused). The account's admins are emailed that the domain is ready. The page then recognises an existing DMARC record ("keep yours") or suggests <code>v=DMARC1; p=none</code>, and says why SPF needs no change: the envelope sender of every message is at the bounce domain, whose SPF record the installation publishes.</li>
</ol>
<p>While it waits, the domain's page looks every 30 seconds and moves on by itself (at most every 20 seconds per domain; a failed automatic look is recorded on the domain, not in the audit log). <em>Check now</em> looks at once and is audited. Every five minutes the host agent also looks at the domains waiting for their DKIM record (each at most every ten minutes). The requests to the host agent appear under Mail flow › Delivery › Host requests. If making a key fails, an admin can try again on the page.</p>
<p>An application can link to a domain's page with the <code>setup_url</code> of the API's sending domains, and add <code>back</code> (an address on its own site) to get a <em>Back to</em> link there.</p>
<p>Until its key is active, a client's domain cannot send on a live installation: the API refuses to submit its jobs. It does not hold up the installation either: the system checks report it as a warning that names the client, never as a failure that blocks live delivery or an upgrade. Only the installation's own domain (that of the sign-in mail) must have its key.</p>
<p>The dashboard never sets up a key for a domain that another client of the installation has also verified, because one key signs a domain for everyone. The operator sets that one up (System setup › DKIM, then <code>smarthost:domain:dkim</code>). Suspended and closed clients add no domains and get no keys. The development pod has no host agent: <code>smarthostctl dev-agent</code> stands in for it there, with the pod's OpenDKIM.</p>
<p>The domain can belong to the client organisation; its DNS records then have to be created by them.</p>
{% endblock %}
